AB  - Over the last two decades, a growing number of ‘Greening of the industry’ initiatives have been launched to adjust industrial designs to meet sustainability demands. Initiatives, referred to as circular economy and industrial symbiosis, have become attractive initiatives to apply into a given industrial setting. The industrial symbiosis builds on the premise that it ""engages traditionally separate entities in a collective approach to competitive advantage involving physical exchange of materials, energy, water, and by-products"" (Chertow, 2000). A symbiosis requires networks to be geographically close and is often initiated and driven by private-public collaborations across organizational borders. The symbiosis networks connect industrial actors wherein heterogenous institutional activities are negotiated, aligned and compromised and generates a space for learning.  Literature on inhabited institutionalism (see e.g., Fine &amp; Hallett, 2014) invites scholars to  recognize the role of institutional logics and also to explore how a specific interaction order shapes the institutional milieu wherein a specific practice is being located. Inhabited institutionalism provides an analytical lens to study groups that mediate both the micro and the macro foundations of institutions, but also has semi-autonomous properties. As ‘greening of the industry’ initiatives connect heterogenous institutional activities, this meso-level theory allows to explore social interactions in greening of the industry initiatives.   The coastal town of Noba (a pseudonym) provides the context for this case study. Noba has a long tradition of fishery. Over time, the fishery industry has expanded, but the emission quotas have remained stagnant due to the increasing environmental impact attributed to the traditional fishery industry. For a coastal village with a historical connection to the industry and aspirations for its continued growth, a reimagining of industrial design has been necessary. This challenge has engaged a variety of stakeholders, including business actors, local municipality actors, and the local community and in engagement to establish industrial symbiosis networks.   This study employs a qualitative research methodology, comprising semi-structured interviews and observations of meetings from ongoing symbiosis projects. Data collection commenced in 2022 and continues to date, with interviews and biweekly meeting observations. The current dataset includes 22 hours of interview material, with 12 hours of interviews conducted with business representatives, symbiosis developers, and municipality actors involved in the symbiosis initiatives, and 10 hours of ongoing updates from project leaders in symbiosis projects. Additionally, 55 hours of meeting observations have been conducted. All interviews and meeting observations have been documented, resulting in 338.5 pages of field material to date.  As institutional pressures penetrate organizations, the practical implications and meanings of these institutional pressures are worked out in social interactions and therefore re-negotiated in settings, in this case a symbiosis network. As such institutional milieu gets established, through e.g., the creation of a symbiosis map that serves as a blueprint for a planned and established symbiosis network, it creates a position and an ability to drive change that would not be accessible within one of the participating public-private organizations.  
